{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1160365489#7_2582746970","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated TeachThought article 'Why Reflection Is Important In Teaching.' It directly supports the need for teachers to review their instruction by explaining that reflection helps teachers confront biases, reconnect with students, re-examine purpose, build agency, and create positive change.","learned":"Reflection can be made part of teaching practice to improve equity, community, purpose, and teachers' ability to change instruction."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q547","reason":"The current query is producing highly relevant documents on why teacher reflection matters; continue to seek additional dedicated results beyond the already judged TeachThought segments."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q547"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1160365489#2_2582738405","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q547","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1160365489#2_2582738405\" , \"end_char\": 2253.000000 , \"headings\": \"Why Reflection Is Important In Teaching<cover>Why Reflection Is Important In Teaching Making Reflection Part Of Your Teaching Practice Reflection As A Strategy To Improve Social Equity Four Strategies For Reflective Teaching Build Community Create a cycle of reflection Choose a Purpose Use Open-Ended Probing Questions About The Author TeachThought Staff \" , \"segment\": \"Without deep thinking about their work, teachers can fall back on rigid policies, mechanical routines, and scripted curricula. To truly challenge this, teachers need to investigate their own values, beliefs, and biases as they work to create transformative and emancipatory learning experiences. That is where reflection comes in. See also 10 Ways To Be A More Reflective Teacher Reflection </cover>has deep philosophical and pedagogical roots, reaching from the works of John Dewey to the field of culturally responsive pedagogy. Reflection is a rigorous and systematic way of thinking that generates new knowledge by helping us create meaning from our experiences. It forces us to ask difficult questions of ourselves and "}}
