{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The direct phrase query 'why teachers should reflect' returned no results to judge.","learned":"That exact wording was too restrictive despite being conceptually aligned."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"reflective practitioner\" teachers (+ teaching instruction) (+ improve improvement) students)","reason":"Search for the established term 'reflective practitioner' in education to find sources explaining why teachers should continually examine and improve their instruction."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"reflective practitioner\" teachers (+ teaching instruction) (+ improve improvement) students)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#3_343639226","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q560","rank":1,"snippet":"Practices and Assessment Skills 6) Connect Positively to the Whole-School Culture \" , \"segment\": \"however, they depend upon other great athletes and coaches to improve their performance over time. If they find themselves at a plateau in their performance, they look for mentors to push them beyond it. But in teaching, a promising young professional can fulfill that expectation without sufficient coaching for years. Of course, there is always teacher evaluation designed for that purpose; still, how often does it truly <cover>improve anything to do with the classroom? Even dedicated supervisors have precious little time to devote to the continuing development of teacher excellence. Teachers can be deceived by what is appealing, habitual and popular with students. Effective teaching is an acquired talent. Professional retreats offer teachers the opportunity to dedicate time to those qualitative steps that result in ongoing development. The six steps to becoming a master teacher include:\" , \"start_char\": 1623.000000 , \"title\": \"Six Steps to Master Teaching: Becoming a Reflective Practitioner | </cover>Edutopia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edutopia.org/blog/master-teaching-margaret-regan\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"19/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#3_343639226","relevance":2,"reason":"The Edutopia article on becoming a reflective practitioner supports the need for teachers to review their instruction by noting that effective teaching is acquired through ongoing development and that teachers can be misled by habitual or popular practices. However, the snippet is more about master teaching and professional development than a document dedicated to why teachers should always review instruction.","learned":"Teachers should review instruction because teaching excellence requires ongoing development and because habits or superficially appealing practices may not improve classroom learning."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q560","reason":"The reflective-practitioner query may surface more direct sections or dedicated sources about reflective teaching and continuous instructional improvement."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q560"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#8_343648890","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q560","rank":2,"snippet":"the classroom; the temporary success of \"fun\" activities can be a hindrance to the development of a master teacher. By crafting performance tasks and assessing them with their peers and mentors, teachers can refine their teaching. 6) Connect Positively to the Whole-School Culture Over time, the master teacher has the capacity to <cover>improve the whole-school culture through excellence in teaching. Because master teaching has as its foundation the generous impulse to assist students and colleagues, the teacher is able to fundamentally influence others without generating resentment. The master teacher is consistently working to benefit the school, so he or she is not in competition with colleagues or administration. In the end, only way to stay the course throughout one's teaching career is by discussion with great teachers who motivate, inspire and remain connected to the classroom. In the company of others, teachers can uncover the best work being done in our schools.\" , \"start_char\": 4406.000000 , \"title\": \"Six Steps to Master Teaching: Becoming a Reflective Practitioner | </cover>Edutopia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edutopia.org/blog/master-teaching-margaret-regan\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"20/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#8_343648890","relevance":2,"reason":"This Edutopia segment supports the broader idea of teachers reviewing and refining instruction by saying teachers can refine teaching with peers and mentors, improve school culture through teaching excellence, and stay connected through discussion with great teachers. However, it is not primarily dedicated to explaining why teachers should always review their instruction.","learned":"Reviewing instruction with peers and mentors can help teachers refine teaching, support school culture, and uncover strong practices."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"continuous improvement\" (+ instruction teaching practice) (+ reflection reflective) \"student learning\")","reason":"Search for continuous-improvement framing, which may yield dedicated evidence that teachers should continually review instruction to improve teaching practice and student learning."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"continuous improvement\" (+ instruction teaching practice) (+ reflection reflective) \"student learning\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_6250596#5_10391822","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q561","rank":1,"snippet":"Incorporate structured, formalized time and resources for coaching, collaboration, and reflection into professional development programs. Encourage teachers to use collaboration and reflection opportunities to explore the relationship between the professional development provided and the impact on <cover>student learning. Use TBTs or communities of learners to provide these opportunities. Back to Top Use teacher evaluation data to inform professional development needs. Align professional development and evaluation in a way that is compliant with the requirements of HB 153 passed by the 129th Ohio General Assembly. Back to Top Why It Is Important Sustained, aligned, intensive professional development that is connected to practice is essential for improving teaching because it provides teachers with time and resources to develop reflective and relevant practices that can lead to mastery of content and pedagogy. In addition to improving teachers’ instructional practice, high-quality professional development can create a more collaborative and more professional school culture. Back to Top 2. Align professional development with the district/school Comprehensive Continuous Improvement </cover>Plan Why It Is Important Aligning professional development with the district continuous improvement plan (CIP) and school improvement plan (SIP) ensures that teachers are working to improve instruction in the areas the district and school have "}}
