{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_373215028#2_520451576","relevance":3,"reason":"The SEDL article is directly about using data to guide instruction and improve student learning, and it explains that teachers should regularly collect and analyze student data, then use it to adjust instruction and plan lessons to ensure effective instruction for students.","learned":"Teachers should review instruction continuously because student data reveals how students are learning and helps teachers adjust instruction and lesson plans to improve learning."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q571","reason":"This query produced a strong grade-3 result on reviewing student data to adjust instruction; continue to find more direct evidence about data-driven instructional review."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q571"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_373215028#3_520453876","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q571","rank":2,"snippet":"2009–2010, SEDL staff worked with district and school leaders in St. Helena Parish School System to sustain systematic improvement and ongoing staff development processes. Data analysis was an integral part of this work. St. Helena’s superintendent had requested that technical assistance focus on literacy instruction, and SEDL confirmed that this was the appropriate target area by reviewing student assessment data and observing teacher planning meetings. The team’s objectives for data use included that all <cover>teachers would engage in quality professional learning at least weekly to ensure delivery of effective instruction for students, and collect student data from several sources—responses on standardized tests, writing samples, and projects—and meet weekly to analyze, interpret, and use the data to adjust instruction </cover>and plan lessons. SEDL staff provided training during which district staff from all content areas learned how to properly implement research-based literacy instructional strategies. In addition, SEDL staff regularly participated in teacher planning meetings and observed classroom instruction to assess the effectiveness of the literacy strategies and provide feedback for improvement. For the 2010–2011 school year, the district leadership team chose Response to Intervention (RtI) to address students’ diverse needs and foster school improvement. Designed "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"31/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_373215028#3_520453876","relevance":3,"reason":"This SEDL article is dedicated to using data to guide instruction and improve student learning. It directly supports reviewing instruction by describing teachers meeting weekly to analyze student data, adjust instruction, plan lessons, observe classroom instruction, assess strategy effectiveness, and receive feedback for improvement.","learned":"Regular instructional review using student data and classroom observations helps teachers assess effectiveness, adjust strategies, plan lessons, and improve learning for diverse student needs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q571","reason":"The data-driven instruction query is yielding dedicated grade-3 evidence about ongoing review of instruction; continue for more directly relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q571"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1618153226#1_3400085386","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q571","rank":3,"snippet":"access daily workflows to help <cover>improve teachers’ workday. Our technology helps teachers to spend less time on administration, more time on education, and to get back time at the end of their day. It also empowers teachers with real-time insights into student progress such that teachers can target growth areas, watch the light bulb turn on, and truly celebrate with students when they show successful growth. When Pennsylvania’s Hatboro-Horsham School District was looking for a new student information system in 2002, its primary goal was a system offering real-time student data and a parent portal for improved communication with families. But what it received was a comprehensive solution that transformed its entire system of business practices and impacted the lives of teachers. “ Teachers save many hours because they no longer have to calculate their standards by hand,” Denise Schlorff, Coordinator of Information Technology. View Case Study Nothing beats knowing you’ve made a difference in the life and education of a child What we find exciting at PowerSchool is that our technology provides easy-to-digest data insights to teachers about their students and classes that enable them to adjust instruction </cover>in real-time. PowerSchool allows "}}
