{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1618153226#1_3400085386","relevance":1,"reason":"The result is related because it says real-time student data helps teachers identify growth areas and adjust instruction, but it is primarily a PowerSchool marketing/product page rather than useful evidence dedicated to why teachers should review instruction.","learned":"Real-time student-progress data can help teachers target growth areas and adjust instruction, but vendor pages are weak evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"formative assessment\" \"adjust instruction\" (+ improve learning students))","reason":"Search for formative assessment, a strong evidence-based rationale for continually reviewing instruction because teachers use ongoing evidence of student learning to adjust teaching."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"formative assessment\" \"adjust instruction\" (+ improve learning students))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1618177656#0_3400137717","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q572","rank":1,"snippet":"formative assessments to gauge student understanding has become more important than ever during COVID-19, when many teachers and students are working in blended learning environments for the first time. But it can be tough to switch from being test-oriented to learning-oriented. Focusing on learning-oriented techniques like formative assessment may be a bit more time consuming and require some extra strategy, but the results are well worth the effort. That’s because these types of diagnostic check-ins are essential to guiding lesson planning and adjusting instruction to support <cover>students’ growth. The Strengths of Formative Assessment When teachers constantly monitor student growth and adjust instruction </cover>to ensure continuous improvement, they find it easier and more predictable to progress towards meeting the standards on summative assessments. By understanding exactly what their students know before and during instruction, educators have much more power to improve student mastery of the subject matter than if they find out after a lesson or unit is complete. The strength of formative assessment lies in the critical information it provides about student comprehension throughout the learning process and the opportunity it gives educators to provide students with timely and action-oriented feedback and to "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"33/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1618177656#0_3400137717","relevance":3,"reason":"The result directly explains why teachers should continually review and adjust instruction through formative assessment: ongoing diagnostic check-ins guide lesson planning, support student growth, ensure continuous improvement, reveal what students know before and during instruction, improve mastery, and enable timely feedback.","learned":"Formative assessment provides ongoing evidence of student comprehension so teachers can adjust instruction during learning rather than waiting until after a unit, improving mastery and continuous progress."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q572","reason":"The formative-assessment query produced strong grade-3 evidence about ongoing instructional review and adjustment; continue to find more directly relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q572"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1693709228#0_3587420168","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q572","rank":2,"snippet":"According to Lang, et al (2008), the formative assessment process came from educators working on ways to accelerate student achievement and help predict how students will perform on standards-based statewide tests. The benefits of formative assessment is obvious in the classroom. The usage of formative assessments in class as a way to guide instruction and to authentically determine student mastery offers many advantages, far beyond higher test scores. According to Chappius & Chappius (2007), although all formative assessment practices have the potential to increase student learning, some of the distinct benefits of <cover>formative assessment ar as follows. Timeliness of results enables teachers to adjust instruction quickly, while learning </cover>is in progress. Students who are assessed are the ones who benefit from the adjustments. Students can use th results to adjust and improve their own learning In very simple terms, formative assessments, unlike summative assessments, allow the student and educator to form a more detailed understanding of the student’s abilities, which can be used to inform remediation, re-teaching, and instructional strategy. With formative assessment, we work with students, we don’t do something to students. We seek to use the data from formative assessments to help the student master "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"34/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1693709228#0_3587420168","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is directly about formative assessment benefits and explains why teachers should keep reviewing instruction: timely results let teachers adjust instruction quickly while learning is still in progress, help students benefit from adjustments, and inform remediation, reteaching, and instructional strategy to improve mastery.","learned":"Ongoing review through formative assessment lets teachers intervene during learning, adjust instruction quickly, reteach or remediate, and help students master content."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q572","reason":"The formative-assessment query is producing strong direct evidence that teachers should continually review instruction using student-learning data; continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q572"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1171079105#1_2404133004","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q572","rank":3,"snippet":"formative years when we draw conclusions about ourselves, a formative assessment is where we begin to draw conclusions about our students' learning. Formative assessment moves can take many forms and generally target skills or content knowledge that is relatively narrow in scope (as opposed to summative assessments, which seek to assess broader sets of knowledge or skills). Common examples of formative assessments include exit tickets, fist-to-five check-ins, teacher-led question-and-answer sessions or games, completed graphic organizers, and practice quizzes. In short, <cover>formative assessment is an essential part of all teaching and learning because it enables teachers to identify and target misunderstandings as they happen, and to adjust instruction </cover>to ensure that all students are keeping pace with the learning goals. As described by the NCTE position paper Formative Assessment That Truly Informs Instruction, formative assessment is a \"constantly occurring process, a verb, a series of events in action, not a single tool or a static noun.\"\" , \"start_char\": 458.000000 , \"title\": \"Teachers' Essential Guide to Formative Assessment | Common Sense Education\" , \"url\": \"https://www.commonsense.org/education/articles/"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"35/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1171079105#1_2404133004","relevance":3,"reason":"The Common Sense Education guide is directly about formative assessment and explains why teachers should continually review instruction: it is essential to teaching and learning because it lets teachers identify misunderstandings as they happen and adjust instruction so all students keep pace with learning goals.","learned":"Teachers should constantly review student understanding because formative assessment reveals misunderstandings in real time and supports immediate instructional adjustment."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q572","reason":"The formative-assessment query is producing strong grade-3 evidence about why teachers should continuously review and adjust instruction; continue to find more direct results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q572"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_494314591#0_1129379228","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q572","rank":4,"snippet":"Getty Images For Students and Parents Homework Help Learning Styles & Skills Homework Tips Study Methods Time Management Private School Test Prep College Admissions College Life Graduate School Business School Law School Distance Learning View More By Derrick Meador Education Expert M.Ed., Educational Administration, Northeastern State University B.Ed., Elementary Education, Oklahoma State University Derrick Meador, M.Ed., is the superintendent for Jennings Public Schools in Oklahoma. He previously served as a school principal and middle school science teacher. our editorial process Derrick Meador Updated March 06, 2017 What is a Formative Assessment? A formative assessment can be defined as a variety of mini-assessments that allow a teacher to <cover>adjust instruction on a frequent basis. These continuous assessments allow teachers to use a variety of instructional strategies to help students reach instructional goals. A formative assessment </cover>is quick and easy to administrator and provides both the teacher and student with quick data that ultimately drives instruction and learning.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Ongoing Formative Assessment Improves Student Learning\" , \"url\": \"https://www.thoughtco.com/what-is-formative-assessment-3194255\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_494314591#0_1129379228","relevance":3,"reason":"The ThoughtCo article is directly titled 'Ongoing Formative Assessment Improves Student Learning' and explains why teachers should continually review instruction: frequent mini-assessments let teachers adjust instruction, use varied strategies to help students reach goals, and use quick data to drive instruction and learning.","learned":"Ongoing formative assessment gives teachers frequent, quick evidence that drives instructional decisions and helps students reach learning goals."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q572","reason":"The formative-assessment query is yielding multiple directly relevant grade-3 results about why teachers should continually review and adjust instruction; continue harvesting this productive result list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q572"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_494314591#1_1129381173","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q572","rank":5,"snippet":"Formative Assessment Can Improve Student Learning What is a Formative Assessment? What is a Formative Assessment? The Benefits of Ongoing Formative Assessment The Best Type of Formative Assessment? Formative Assessments vs. Summative Assessments Wrapping It Up \" , \"segment\": \"He previously served as a school principal and middle school science teacher. our editorial process Derrick Meador Updated March 06, 2017 What is a Formative Assessment? A formative assessment can be defined as a variety of mini-assessments that allow a teacher to <cover>adjust instruction on a frequent basis. These continuous assessments allow teachers to use a variety of instructional strategies to help students reach instructional goals. A formative assessment </cover>is quick and easy to administrator and provides both the teacher and student with quick data that ultimately drives instruction and learning. Formative assessments focus on an individual skill or a subset of skills within a curriculum instead of the entire curriculum. These assessments are intended to measure progress towards a specific goal. They also provide students with a deeper understanding of skills they have mastered as well as skills they struggle with. There are many different types of formative assessments that can be used in any "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_494314591#1_1129381173","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated ThoughtCo article on ongoing formative assessment improving student learning. It directly explains that continuous mini-assessments let teachers adjust instruction frequently, choose strategies to help students reach goals, and use quick data to drive instruction and learning.","learned":"Continuous review through formative assessment helps teachers monitor specific skills, identify mastered and struggling areas, and adjust instruction based on timely data."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q572","reason":"The formative-assessment result list is highly productive for direct evidence that teachers should always review instruction; continue to collect more grade-3 results while it remains useful."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q572"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1171079105#2_2404135306","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q572","rank":6,"snippet":"formative years when we draw conclusions about ourselves, a formative assessment is where we begin to draw conclusions about our students' learning. Formative assessment moves can take many forms and generally target skills or content knowledge that is relatively narrow in scope (as opposed to summative assessments, which seek to assess broader sets of knowledge or skills). Common examples of formative assessments include exit tickets, fist-to-five check-ins, teacher-led question-and-answer sessions or games, completed graphic organizers, and practice quizzes. In short, <cover>formative assessment is an essential part of all teaching and learning because it enables teachers to identify and target misunderstandings as they happen, and to adjust instruction </cover>to ensure that all students are keeping pace with the learning goals. As described by the NCTE position paper Formative Assessment That Truly Informs Instruction, formative assessment is a \"constantly occurring process, a verb, a series of events in action, not a single tool or a static noun.\" What makes a good formative assessment? As mentioned above, formative assessments can take many forms. The most useful formative assessments share some common traits: They assess skills and content that have been derived from the backward planning process. They "}}
